{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>Flee for your life.</b> It is enough for you to save lives — have no pity on the property." "<b>Do not look behind you.</b> You sinned with them, and by the merit of Abraham you are saved; you are not worthy to look upon their punishment while you are being saved." "<b>Through all the plain.</b> The plain of the Jordan." "<b>To the mountain, flee.</b> Flee to Abraham, for he dwells in the mountain, as it says ״וַיַּעְתֵּק מִשָּׁם הָהָרָה״ (Genesis 12), and even now he was dwelling there, as it says ״to the place where his tent had been at first״ — and even though it is written ״וַיֶּאֱהַל אַבְרָהָם״ etc. (ibid. 13), he had many tents and they extended as far as Hebron." "<b>Flee.</b> A term of slipping away; and so every הַמְלָטָה in Scripture — אשמוצי״ר בְּלַעַ״ז. And likewise ״וְהִמְלִיטָה זָכָר״ (Isaiah 66) — that the fetus slips from the womb; ״כְּצִפּוֹר נִמְלְטָה״ (Psalms 124); ״לֹא יָכְלוּ מַלֵּט מַשָּׂא״ (Isaiah 46) — to slip off the load of dung that is in their bowels."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {4 "the la'az אשמוצי״ר is Old French (s')eschmo(u)vir, 'to slip away / escape'"}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:59:31.288618147Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}